"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[75845],{198226:e=>{e.exports="# SaaS Connector Diagnostics\n\n## Common Issues\n\n### Credential types\n\nSaaS connectors usually use one of: OAuth (refresh token / connected app), API key, or username + password + token. Identify which type the connection uses before diagnosing auth failures — the fix differs significantly.\n\n### Unknown host / DNS resolution failure\n\nAn \"Unknown Host\" or \"Name or service not known\" error means the pipeline could not resolve the SaaS endpoint's hostname. This most commonly happens when the workspace has a **Secure Egress Gateway (SEG)** configured — the SEG controls which external hostnames serverless compute can reach, and the source system's domain is not on the allow-list. Ask the user to verify with their workspace administrator that SEG is configured to allow outbound access to the source system's hostname.\n\n### IP allowlisting\n\nMany SaaS platforms restrict API access by IP. A 403 with no other explanation (no \"permission denied\" on a specific object) often means Databricks' outbound NAT IPs are not on the source system's allowlist. Ask the user to check their source system's network/security settings and add the Databricks cloud NAT IP ranges for their region.\n\n### Integration user permissions\n\nSaaS connectors authenticate as a single service or integration user. That user must have **read access to every object being ingested** — API-level authentication is not sufficient on its own. Verify object-level permissions in the source system for each table, report, or dataset that is failing.\n\n### Rate limiting\n\n429 responses or \"rate limit exceeded\" errors mean the connector is hitting the source API's throttle limits. Ask the user to check the source system's API rate limit settings, reduce the number of selected objects, or reduce sync frequency.\n"}}]);
